{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe plan-review attestation currency check `rebar review-plan <id> --status` is a read-only,\nno-LLM, no-network query that answers \"should I re-gate before I implement?\". It is exported\nfrom the library (`rebar.llm.plan_review_status`) and exposed on the CLI, but there is NO MCP\ntool for it. An MCP-only agent can therefore never ASK whether its attestation is current — it\nonly discovers staleness by attempting a `claim` or a `transition ... closed` and reading the\nresulting error.\n\nFound while sweeping every attestation refusal surface for bug 94a3-1323-3497-4f59, which made\nthe refusal messages name what changed. That sweep helps a CLI agent immediately; an MCP agent\nstill has to provoke a refusal to learn anything, which is the parity gap.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd a read-only MCP tool `plan_review_status` alongside the existing read tools in\n`src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py`, registered by `register_read_tools` with the `READ_ONLY`\nannotation preset (so it is present under `REBAR_MCP_READONLY` and absent from the write\nregistrar). Its body is a thin delegation to `rebar.llm.plan_review_status(ticket_id)` —\nthe SAME function the CLI's `--status` branch calls — imported lazily inside the tool body\nso the `[agents]`-extra import cost is not paid at read-registrar import time and so the\ndelegation is observable (patching `rebar.llm.plan_review_status` changes the tool's answer).\n\nIts return annotation is a new `PlanReviewStatusOut` pydantic model in\n`src/rebar/_mcp_models.py` (where every other MCP output model lives — the ticket's original\nscope guessed `src/rebar/schemas/`, but the JSON-Schema files there are only needed for the\nsubset of models listed in `check_mcp_models.MODEL_SCHEMAS`, which this additive model does\nnot join). The model declares the documented\n`{ok, verdict, reason, verified_at_sha, signed_at}` shape and inherits the permissive\n`extra=\"allow\"` base, following `VerifySignatureResultOut`.\n\n`docs/mcp-reference.md` is GENERATED by `scripts/gen_mcp_reference.py` from the server's own\nregistrars and is guarded by a CI drift gate, so the doc row is produced by regenerating the\nfile, not by hand-editing it.\n\nWhy this rather than widening an existing tool: `show_ticket` already carries a derived\n`plan_review_health` payload, but that is relationship/phase health, not the claim-gate\ncurrency verdict — overloading it would conflate two different questions and change an\nexisting tool's contract. A dedicated read-only tool is additive and matches how the CLI\nalready separates the two.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py` — the new read-only `plan_review_status` tool.\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_models.py` — the `PlanReviewStatusOut` output model.\n- `docs/mcp-reference.md` — regenerated so the read-tool inventory lists the tool.\n- `tests/unit/test_mcp_plan_review_status.py` — the new tests.\n\nBehaviour-preserving for every existing surface: no existing tool, model, CLI verb, or\nlibrary function is modified; this change is purely additive.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Happy path: a ticket with a current attestation returns `ok: true`, `verdict: \"certified\"`.\n- Failure path: a ticket with no attestation returns `ok: false`, `verdict: \"unsigned\"`.\n- Delegation: patching `rebar.llm.plan_review_status` changes the tool's output.\n- The tool is registered in the MCP read-tool set and is absent from the write set (it must\n  not be gated by readonly mode).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 — A read-only MCP tool named `plan_review_status` is registered by\n      `register_read_tools` and is NOT registered by `register_write_tools`.\n      Verify: `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_mcp_plan_review_status.py -q -k inventory`\n- [ ] AC2 — For a ticket whose plan-review attestation is current the tool returns\n      `ok: true` and `verdict: \"certified\"`.\n      Verify: `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_mcp_plan_review_status.py -q -k certified`\n- [ ] AC3 — For a ticket with no plan-review attestation the tool returns `ok: false` and\n      `verdict: \"unsigned\"`.\n      Verify: `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_mcp_plan_review_status.py -q -k unsigned`\n- [ ] AC4 — The tool delegates to `rebar.llm.plan_review_status` rather than reimplementing\n      the check; patching that function changes the tool's output.\n      Verify: `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_mcp_plan_review_status.py -q -k delegat`\n- [ ] AC5 — `docs/mcp-reference.md` lists `plan_review_status` in its Read-only section and\n      is not stale with respect to its generator.\n      Verify: `grep -q '`plan_review_status`' docs/mcp-reference.md && python scripts/gen_mcp_reference.py --check`\n- [ ] AC6 — No existing behaviour regresses: lint, typecheck, and the MCP + doc-generator\n      unit tests pass.\n      Verify: `make lint && make typecheck && python -m pytest tests/unit -q -k \"mcp\"`"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786497179130593001,"uuid":"fbe8ee2b-ba3f-4bd6-a66d-eb655efa90d7"}